ld look familiar  um i just want to say that that  something i glossed over before is this  shaded region here  that's a 95 confidence interval so like  i said we we pulled the data from  the fda's website out of these the data  they report  the manufacturers reported these are  finite sample sizes so  we didn't just calculate the expected  ppv in npv but we could also put  a 95 confidence interval on it  and so here you can select test there's  dozens of manufacturers  i'm going to go with with abbott here  they're pretty popular  and then from there you can select  whichever tests they've submitted to the  fda  and here you'll notice we've got one  test but then multiple  sources where the source says find dx so  the  a couple speakers ago we had a couple  speakers from find  we've been supplementing the data that  we're pulling out of the pdfs with  data that find is collected that's often  um you know  independent evaluations that maybe have  larger sample sizes than  what the manufacturer reported to the  fda but here i'm going to go with this  uh abbott uh a g card this is a  an antigen rapid test it's received a  bit of press recently  on the left you've got this positive  predictive value chart for  the abbot antigen test on the right  we've included data  from an influenza rapid diagnostic test  the purpose here is so that you can see  how does my coronavirus test compare to  a flu test that is you know used and  trusted  uh regularly so you can see the overall  shape of these curves are the same  like i said this this uh uh the main  properties here are properties of  testing for relatively rare conditions  not anything unique to coronavirus but  the coronavirus test has a much wider  confidence interval because there's  more data backing up the influenza test  if you scroll down for those that may be  less mathematically inclined  we've got these text boxes here that say  for the selected prevalence level if you  give a hundred thousand tests here's how  